/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> feetOstinatosLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "An ostinato is a foot pattern that never changes while the hands do something else. Bossa nova, samba and jazz all rest on one: once the feet run on autopilot, the hands are free to play the clave, the samba sticking or the comping above them.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Let the feet play the pattern alone until it runs without thinking — right foot heel up for the kick, left foot heel down on the hi-hat pedal — and then add the hands one at a time.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Lines 1 and 2 are bossa nova: kick on 1, \"2 +\", 3, \"4 +\", the cross-stick clave on top. Lines 3 and 4 are samba: kick on 1 and the \"a\" of every beat, hi-hat foot on 2 and 4, then the hands play the accented samba sticking on the snare. Lines 5–8 are jazz: feathered quarters on the kick, chick on 2 and 4, ride pattern and comping on top.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Antônio Carlos Jobim and João Gilberto \"The Girl from Ipanema\" and \"Desafinado\" (bossa), Airto Moreira and Duduka Da Fonseca (samba on the kit), and the jazz ride-and-foot feel of Jimmy Cobb and Elvin Jones.",
  ),
];
